---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc815.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — the Definition of a Derivative" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Embedded Derivatives — Bifurcation" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Key Divergence — IFRS 9 Eliminated Bifurcation for Financial Asset Hosts" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Three Types of Hedges" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Hedge Effectiveness — the ASU 2017-12 Simplification" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Fair Value Hedge Accounting" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Cash Flow Hedge Accounting" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Net Investment Hedges" },
        { id: "s10", label: "The Normal Purchases and Normal Sales Exception" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Worked Example — Fair Value Hedge Accounting" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Worked Example — Cash Flow Hedge Accounting" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Worked Example — the Embedded Derivative Bifurcation Divergence" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s16", label: "ASC 815 vs IFRS 9 — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s18", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s19", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Derivatives are recognised at fair value — a converged foundational principle</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 815's foundational rule — shared FULLY with IFRS 9 — is that ALL derivative instruments are recognised on the balance sheet at FAIR VALUE, as either assets or liabilities. HEDGE ACCOUNTING then exists as a special, elective set of rules allowing the TIMING of gain/loss recognition on qualifying hedging relationships to better MATCH the timing of the hedged item's own income statement effect.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">Complex, but with converged bones and one major structural gap</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">This is one of the most operationally complex Topics in the entire Codification — but its BROAD hedge accounting categories and general philosophy are closely aligned with IFRS 9. The single most significant STRUCTURAL divergence is embedded derivative bifurcation for financial asset hosts (Section 4), stemming from IFRS 9's broader 2014 classification overhaul that US GAAP never paralleled.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — the Definition of a Derivative</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Underlying, notional, minimal initial investment, net settlement</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">A DERIVATIVE has: (a) one or more UNDERLYINGS (e.g. an interest rate, price index) and one or more NOTIONAL amounts or payment provisions; (b) requires NO initial net investment, or one SMALLER than would be required for other contracts expected to have a similar response to market factors; and (c) its terms REQUIRE OR PERMIT net settlement, or provide for delivery of an asset readily convertible to cash — the same general definitional framework IFRS 9 uses.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Embedded Derivatives — Bifurcation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The "clearly and closely related" test</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An EMBEDDED derivative (a derivative feature contained within a larger "host" contract, e.g. a convertible bond with an embedded conversion option) must be SEPARATED ("bifurcated") from its host contract and accounted for SEPARATELY at fair value if: the economic characteristics of the embedded feature are NOT "clearly and closely related" to the host contract; a SEPARATE instrument with the same terms would qualify as a derivative; and the hybrid instrument is NOT already measured entirely at fair value through earnings.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Key Divergence — IFRS 9 Eliminated Bifurcation for Financial Asset Hosts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A structural gap stemming from IFRS 9's broader classification overhaul</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IFRS 9 (unlike its predecessor IAS 39) ELIMINATED embedded derivative bifurcation ENTIRELY for FINANCIAL ASSET host contracts — a HYBRID financial asset (e.g. a convertible bond HELD as an investment) is instead classified in its ENTIRETY under IFRS 9's classification model, using the SPPI ("solely payments of principal and interest") cash flow characteristics test. IFRS 9 RETAINS bifurcation ONLY for financial LIABILITY hosts and NONFINANCIAL host contracts. US GAAP (ASC 815) CONTINUES to require the "clearly and closely related" bifurcation analysis for ALL types of host contracts, including financial ASSET hosts — a genuine, structural divergence stemming directly from IFRS 9's broader 2014 classification overhaul, which US GAAP simply never paralleled in the same way for financial assets.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Three Types of Hedges</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">The same three categories used under IFRS 9</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Hedge type</span><span>What it addresses</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Fair value hedge</span><span>Hedging exposure to changes in the fair value of a recognised asset/liability or firm commitment</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Cash flow hedge</span><span>Hedging exposure to variability in cash flows from a forecasted transaction or variable-rate instrument</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Net investment hedge</span><span>Hedging foreign currency exposure on a net investment in a foreign operation</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Hedge Effectiveness — the ASU 2017-12 Simplification</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Both frameworks have moved toward principles-based effectiveness testing</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">ASU 2017-12 significantly SIMPLIFIED US hedge accounting — allowing QUALITATIVE effectiveness assessments in many cases (rather than mandatory quantitative testing), and eliminating separate measurement/reporting of hedge INEFFECTIVENESS for many cash flow and net investment hedges. IFRS 9's OWN hedge accounting model (introduced 2018) is ALSO principles-based, having dropped IAS 39's old rigid 80-125% bright-line effectiveness range test — both frameworks have moved in the SAME general direction toward more flexible, principles-based hedge effectiveness assessment.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Fair Value Hedge Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Both sides through earnings, ideally offsetting</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The HEDGED ITEM's carrying amount is ADJUSTED for changes in fair value attributable to the HEDGED RISK, recognised in EARNINGS TOGETHER WITH the hedging instrument's own fair value change (also through earnings) — the two ideally OFFSETTING each other, leaving only genuine ineffectiveness as a net income statement impact. This mirrors IFRS 9's own fair value hedge model.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Cash Flow Hedge Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">OCI deferral, with eventual recycling — genuinely converged</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The EFFECTIVE portion of the hedging instrument's gain/loss is DEFERRED in OCI, and RECLASSIFIED into earnings when the HEDGED forecasted transaction itself affects earnings — the SAME OCI-deferral-with-eventual-RECYCLING mechanic IFRS 9 uses for cash flow hedges. Notably, this is one of the areas in the library where BOTH frameworks genuinely use OCI WITH recycling — unlike some other "permanent OCI" areas discussed elsewhere.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Net Investment Hedges</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Deferred within the cumulative translation adjustment</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Gains/losses on instruments hedging a NET INVESTMENT in a foreign operation are DEFERRED within the CUMULATIVE TRANSLATION ADJUSTMENT component of OCI — converged with IFRS 9's own net investment hedge treatment.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>The Normal Purchases and Normal Sales Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A broadly converged scope exception</div><div style="font-size:var(--text-small);color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Contracts for the purchase/sale of a commodity in the NORMAL course of business, expected to be used or sold over a REASONABLE period, are EXCLUDED from derivative accounting (not marked to market) if SPECIFIC criteria are met and the exception is properly DOCUMENTED/elected at inception. IFRS 9 has a broadly similar "OWN USE" exception for contracts entered into for the entity's own purchase/sale/usage requirements — a genuinely converged concept, though with some differing documentation/election mechanics.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Fair Value Hedge Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Offsetting gains and losses through earnings</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Hedging fixed-rate debt with an interest rate swap</div><div class="wk-body">An entity hedges $10,000,000 of fixed-rate debt with a receive-fixed, pay-variable interest rate swap, designated as a fair value hedge. Interest rates rise, causing the swap's fair value to DECREASE by $200,000, while the debt's fair value ALSO decreases by $195,000 (attributable to the hedged interest rate risk).</div><div class="wk-answer">Debit Loss on swap (earnings) $200,000 / Credit Swap liability $200,000. Debit Debt (carrying amount adjustment) $195,000 / Credit Gain on hedged item (earnings) $195,000. Net income statement impact = $200,000 loss minus $195,000 gain = <strong>$5,000 net loss</strong>, representing genuine hedge INEFFECTIVENESS — the two largely OFFSET, as intended.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — Cash Flow Hedge Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Deferral in OCI, then reclassification</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Hedging a forecasted commodity purchase</div><div class="wk-body">An entity enters a commodity forward contract to hedge a forecasted raw material purchase occurring in 6 months. The forward's fair value increases by $80,000 by the hedge designation date's next reporting period.</div><div class="wk-answer">Debit Forward contract (asset) $80,000 / Credit OCI $80,000 — the gain is DEFERRED, not recognised in earnings yet. When the forecasted purchase actually OCCURS 6 months later and the raw material is eventually SOLD as part of finished goods, the $80,000 is RECLASSIFIED out of OCI into COST OF GOODS SOLD (or wherever the hedged transaction's effect on earnings occurs) — matching the timing of the hedge's benefit to the timing of the underlying transaction's income statement effect.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example — the Embedded Derivative Bifurcation Divergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">A convertible bond held as an investment</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An investor holding a convertible bond</div><div class="wk-body">An entity purchases a convertible bond issued by another company as an investment — a hybrid instrument with an embedded equity conversion option, held as a FINANCIAL ASSET.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Under US GAAP (ASC 815):</strong> the entity must analyse whether the embedded conversion option is "clearly and closely related" to the debt host — it likely is NOT, so the conversion option must be BIFURCATED and accounted for SEPARATELY as a derivative at fair value through earnings, with the debt host accounted for separately.<br><strong>Under IFRS 9:</strong> NO bifurcation analysis is performed at all for this FINANCIAL ASSET host — the ENTIRE hybrid instrument is instead classified in its ENTIRETY based on the SPPI test; since the conversion feature means the instrument's cash flows are NOT solely principal and interest, the WHOLE instrument is simply classified at FVTPL, with NO separate embedded derivative ever recognised.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 815 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Objectives and strategies for using derivative instruments, and their risk management purpose</span><span>Fair value of derivative instruments, by risk category and hedge designation, and their location on the balance sheet</span><span>Gains/losses recognised on derivatives, and the income statement line items affected</span><span>Amount expected to be reclassified from OCI into earnings within the next 12 months for cash flow hedges</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an embedded feature is "clearly and closely related" to its host contract</span><span>Whether a contract qualifies for the normal purchases and normal sales exception</span><span>Hedge designation and documentation at inception</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of derivative instruments, particularly complex or customised ones</span><span>Assessment of hedge effectiveness, whether qualitative or quantitative</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>ASC 815 vs IFRS 9 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 815 vs IFRS 9's derivative and hedging provisions</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 815)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 9)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Embedded derivative bifurcation — financial asset hosts</span><span>REQUIRED — clearly and closely related test applies universally</span><span>ELIMINATED — hybrid financial assets classified in their entirety under the SPPI test instead</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Embedded derivative bifurcation — liability/nonfinancial hosts</span><span>Required</span><span>Also required — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Hedge effectiveness testing</span><span>Qualitative assessment permitted since ASU 2017-12; no rigid bright-line ratio</span><span>Principles-based since 2018 IFRS 9 hedge accounting model; no rigid 80-125% ratio</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Fair value hedge mechanics</span><span>Both sides through earnings</span><span>Same — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Cash flow hedge mechanics</span><span>OCI deferral, recycled to earnings</span><span>Same — converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Normal purchases/normal sales (own use) exception</span><span>Available, with specific documentation requirements</span><span>Available, broadly similar concept</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The embedded derivative bifurcation gap for financial asset hosts is the single most consequential structural divergence remaining — it stems directly from IFRS 9's 2014 classification overhaul, which fundamentally changed how hybrid financial ASSETS are analysed under IFRS in a way US GAAP never mirrored.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 815 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Derivative fair value change, no hedge designation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised immediately in earnings</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Fair value hedge</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Both hedging instrument and hedged item through earnings</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash flow hedge (effective portion)</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Deferred, reclassified when the hedged transaction affects earnings</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Net investment hedge</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Within cumulative translation adjustment</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Bifurcated embedded derivative</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised separately at fair value through earnings</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 220</span><span>Governs the OCI presentation framework cash flow and net investment hedge deferrals flow through</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 480</span><span>Governs classification of certain equity-linked instruments intersecting with embedded derivative analysis</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 470</span><span>Debt — supplies the host contract guidance embedded derivative bifurcation is layered onto for convertible debt</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Financial Instruments — IFRS counterpart for derivative instruments and hedge accounting</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Embedded derivative</div><div class="glossary-def">A derivative feature contained within a larger host contract, requiring separate bifurcated accounting under specific criteria.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Clearly and closely related test</div><div class="glossary-def">The test determining whether an embedded derivative's economic characteristics are sufficiently aligned with its host contract to avoid bifurcation.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC815 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

